The product ordered at Tsukumo Online Shop was replaced without permission.
Summary: When I ordered a cheap wireless mouse, the wired version of the mouse arrived, and if I wanted a refund, I was told to send it back.
It's not a self-made PC topic, it's a mail-order problem, but no matter how you think about it, it's not acceptable and it's a famous place, so I'm going to write it
MSI's wireless gaming mouse was sold for 3,280yen at Tsukumo's Tsukumo Day sale, which started last Friday.
Since the wired version was the same price at the store, considering the possibility of a mistake in the price and checking the wired version's page, it was 5,071yen (link destination Uotaku), so I ordered it considering the possibility that it was not a mistake
I received an order e-mail normally, and I also received an e-mail shipped the next day.
In these two e-mails and the purchase history of My Page, the name of "CLUTCH GM41 LIGHTWEIGHT WIRELESS" was written.
Since there was no notification of error or cancellation from the order to shipment, I thought that the wireless mouse I ordered would arrive at this point.
It was a problem from here, and a few hours after the shipment, I received an e-mail saying that there was a mistake in the registration of the product.
According to the e-mail.
* The wireless version was sold at a special sale price, but in fact it was a wired version
* Since the wired mouse has already been shipped,
1. All cancellations after refusing to receive
2. Refund by returning only the mouse
3. Use a wired mouse as it is
Choose your response.
The product page of the wired version and the wireless version are separated, and although it is written as a wireless version in the purchase history, e-mail, and delivery slip, it seems that it was sent without permission to the wired version.
If you can send something that is different from the product you bought without an understanding, and if you want a refund, is it bad to send it back from here?
In the end, even if you accept the refund, I want to fight back with one shot, so please lend me your wisdom.
