Isolated twice ... the only bridge that leads to the district due to typhoons and heavy rains leaked Residents triple the stockpiled food (Fukushima Yatsuri Town> (Fukushima TV) - Yahoo! News
Mr. Yoshiro MATSUMOTO in the Takachihara area of Yasai-cho, Fukushima Prefecture.
It was the typhoon in eastern Japan that had a major impact on the lives of eight people in three households.
Yoshiro Matsumoto: "At first, it's like, 'I'm serious,' isn't it?
I wondered if it would break so quickly, and I wonder if it would be swept away."The only road leading to the Takachihara area was washed away by the typhoon in eastern Japan, and thirty people from eleven households in the district became isolated.
The residents, who had been forced to cut off the water, continued to carry the necessary supplies using JR's railway bridges, which are not normally allowed to pass.
Mr. Yoshiro Matsumoto: "Usually, cars can come to the garden, but when I get out of the car there, I hold it in my hand or carry it on my back and walk through the iron bridge, so I felt the means of transportation and the significance of the car."
More than two years have passed since the disaster, but Mr. Matsumoto continues to take advantage of his lessons.
Mr. Yoshiro Matsumoto: "I think there are about ten cases.
For the time being, I would like to make sure that I can somehow secure 2-3 days." Foods that can be used for emergency meals, such as canned food and cup noodles, should be prepared about three times more than before the disaster.
Misako Matsumoto "I don't know when a disaster will fall on me, not someone else's business, so I felt again that it would be better for everyone to do a little stockpile."
In order not to remember the lesson, the town started its own efforts from 2021.
Ryuichi TAKAHASHI, chief of the Yamatsuri-cho Education Division, said, "There is also an announcement in the form of 'Yamatsuri-cho Disaster Prevention Day,' in which we hope that October 12th will be a day to think about disaster preparedness after next year.
More than ever before, I think I’m becoming more aware of what’s going to happen.”
