Be careful when buying second-hand homes!
What are the pitfalls of the "building situation survey system" (Diamond Online) - Yahoo! News
For the revitalization of the second-hand housing market Newly established "building situation survey" system The second-hand housing trading is strong.
Prices for both detached houses and condominiums are rising, and the remodeling market is also expanding.
On the other hand, the popularity of properties represented by words such as "stations, stations and stations", "large-scale", and "towers" that are soaring is concentrated, and the number of relatively cheap new buildings has drastically decreased.
[See the image in this article] However, compared to overseas, the share of existing (used) housing distribution in Japan is still at a low level.
Therefore, the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism (Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism) partially revised the Real Estate Transaction Business Act in 2018, and launched a survey on the status of buildings for the purpose of transactions of safe and secure second-hand houses, a so-called inspection (https://www.mlit.go.jp/common/001219900.pdf).
This is a system in which an architect who is familiar with housing checks the current situation and the condition of the building, including the deterioration of the property, summarizes it in a report, and presents it to the buyer.
The Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism explained that the introduction of inspection has benefits for both buyers and sellers.
This is because the seller can avoid post-delivery claims by conducting a property survey before buying and selling transactions, giving the buyer a sense of security.
In addition, it will lead to the differentiation of competing properties.
On the other hand, it is a system that can grasp the state of the second-handed house to be purchased and can be purchased convincingly for the buyer's side, and at first glance it is a system with a big advantage.
However, under the system of the current system, it is mainly the seller who has the initiative in the building situation survey.
In addition, I would like to pay attention to the fact that it has become an obligation only to announce the inspection system.
In other words, there is an obligation to explain that the real estate business operator in charge of brokerage can introduce an architect who conducts a building situation survey at the time of property brokerage, or that it is not possible, but the implementation itself is not mandatory.
On the other hand, if you look overseas, similar systems have already been developed in the United States and the United Kingdom, etc., where used markets are booming.
In the pre-sale stage of housing, it is a system in which the buyer asks an expert called “inspector” or “Building Surveyor” to conduct a building survey.
For example, in the case of the U.S., 70 to 90 percent of second-hand housing transactions, depending on the state, are subject to inspection.
In terms of "buyers" taking the lead and conducting inspections, the Japanese-style inspection and its nature are very different.
The seller tries to show the condition of the building as much as possible and sells as soon as possible.
On the other hand, the buyer wants to study the contents of the survey and live with peace of mind with appropriate repairs.
Even if it is the same inspection, there is a gap in the purpose and thoughts of both sides.
Therefore, with regard to Japan's system, the following points have been pointed out as risks of concern.
● The scope of the survey of seller-driven inspections determined by the government shall be kept to a minimum.
There is a risk of overlooking really important defects because it is not a detailed investigation ● For an inspection company, an intermediary company (seller) who will introduce the case in charge can be said to be a customer.
Therefore, there is a possibility that "intentional" inspection will be carried out, such as not reporting unfavorable findings to the seller.
