We will introduce Ginza's trivia that it is interesting to know and want to talk to someone.
Going over Ginza! ?
Immediately infiltrated Kanaza
When you walk through Ginza, you will find remnants of the history of Ginza, including quirky place names, mysterious stone monuments, and numerous shrines.
When I was walking with her in Ginza, the silence that came unexpectedly said, "It used to be a peninsula here.
Was Ginza once a peninsula?
“Ginza” in Chuo-ku, Tokyo, is synonymous with the downtown area that created “○○ Ginza” in various parts of Japan.
In the first place, "Ginza" means the minting of silver coins, and in the 1612s, the current Ginza Office in Shizuoka City was moved to Edo.
At that time, it was said to be a "new currency exchange town", but when it moved to Togakusaku-cho, it came to be called "Ginza".
By the way, it is said that the area around Ginza used to be a "penson" that protruded into the sea.
Before the Edo period, there were covees from Hibiya to Marunouchi, and from Nihonbashi to Ginza, a peninsula called "Edomae Island" grew.
The tip of the peninsula is the current Ginza.
When Ieyasu TOKUGAWA opened the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) in Edo, he reclaimed an irie and promoted the development of the Kyobashi area.
In addition, Ieyasu built a district, and in order to develop boat transportation, he went around Horiwari.
Then, he cut off the soil and Mt. Kanda, where he cut down the moat, and buried the cove.
In front of Tiffany Ginza Main Store (2-7 Ginza, Chuo-ku), there is a stone monument of "the birthplace of Ginza" indicating the site of the Ginza government office, so why don't you look for the direction you are interested.
Those who know, know the "pilgrimage to the sacred place" of Ginza
Every year, a "pilgrimage to a sacred place" known to everyone who knows "Ginza Hatcho Shrine Tour" is held in Ginza.
Of the more than ten sacred sites, the easiest to see is "Kabuki Inari Shrine" in front of the Kabuki-za Theater.
There are many people who visit the shrine on the right side of the Kabuki-za Theater before and after the theater.
On the other hand, "Ryumitsu Fudoson" is on the rooftop of Ginza Matsuya, and "Ginza Kisei Jizoson" is located on the Ginza Terrace on the 9th floor of Ginza Mitsukoshi.
"Hodo Inari Shrine", "Azuma Inari Shrine" and "Toyoiwa Inari Shrine" are located at the back of the alley, and it is difficult to stand out, but "Toyoiwa Inari Shrine" is known for reveration by Kabuki famous actor Uzaemon Ichimura, and "Success Inari Shrine" at the entrance of Shiseido Ginza Building 1F is not open to the public except when visiting Hachicho-jinja Shrine.
There are places where Fudo Myoo and Jisguro Bodhisattva are enshrined in this shrine tour, but most of the shrines have Inari as enshrined deities.
"Inari-sha Shrine," in which the fox welcomes you in front of the main shrine, was erected in Sakan, where there are many farmers, because there is the benefit of a "bountiful harvest of five grains" in which rice is often mixed.
In large cities such as Kyoto and Edo, it has come to be said to be beneficial for the prosperity of business, and even in Ginza, Japan's busiest downtown area, faith has become widespread.
