Let's think about "Tamano who wants to continue to live" Four high school students' workshops in the city to recommendations: Sanyo Shimbun Digital|SANDIGI
What kind of town is Tamano in 2030?
Student representatives from four high schools in Tamano City are working together on a workshop to think about "Tamano who wants to continue to live".
As part of the research and policy recommendation project of the interim support organization "Tamano SDGs Mirai Creation Center", we will summarize the voices of young people and make recommendations to the mayors.
“When are you having fun?”
In response to the facilitator’s questions, the students answered questions such as “when you get excited with a lot of people” and “when you play the drums”.
On November 13th, it was held for the first time at Hinode Fureai Kaikan.
Eighteen high school students and members of the local community talked about the "world cafe" method while moving the "island" where the theme was decided, thinking about "an interesting town", "an easy-to-livable town", and "a town where you can feel the purpose of life".
Write down each idea on a sticky note, paste it on imitation paper, and organize it.
"It looks interesting in a town with a lot of young people" "Traffic such as trains and buses is indispensable for livability".
Let’s have nothing in common or discuss more.
"I thought Tamano was boring, but it's attractive to be able to cycle by the seaside," said Tamano Shoko 3-year-old student (18).
Binan high school third-year student (18) said, "There are few shops, traffic is inconvenient, but it is good where the nature is rich," and by talking with adults, he seemed to notice not only the issues of the region but also the charm.
In the second session in December, only high school students gathered and organized the contents of the recommendations.
In February of next year, the mayor and the head of education will be invited to make an announcement.
Rie Higashi, president of the center, said, "We hope to foster the love of high school students in their hometowns and that it will be an opportunity for young people to be actively involved in community development."
