Unseasonable warmth and effects of strong cold (Weather News) - Yahoo! News
From the 10th (Friday) and 11th (Saturday) of the local time, there were many tornadoes mainly in the southern part of the United States.
During this period, strong cold air moved southward, which was a record warmth, and the state of the atmosphere became very unstable, and it seems that cumulonimbus clouds developed rapidly.
In the southern part of the U.S., warm air flowed from the southwest, resulting in a record high temperature.
In Houston, Texas, the maximum temperature on the 9th (Thursday) and the 10th (Friday) has risen to around 30oC.
At such a record-grossing place, a strong cold air below -30°C was moving southward around 5500 metres above, so an active front was formed due to a large temperature difference, and cumulonimbus clouds developed rapidly along the front.
It seems that tornadoes occurred one after another in the vicinity.
The southern part of the U.S. is originally an area prone to tornadoes, but from early summer to summer, it is a high season, and there are not many cases of major damage at this time.
The tornado alert, which was released in one day during the winter season, reached nearly 150 times and was a record-high number.
More than four hundred tornadoes and gusty winds were reported by the U.S. Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration on Friday.
According to this, we can see that there are two concentrated areas: the southern line from Kentucky to Texas and the northern line, including Illinois.
In Kentucky, the EF scale (improved Fujita scale), which shows the strength of the tornado, reached the EF3, which is the 3rd strongest of the six stages, and it is thought that a gust of around 70m / s blew at the moment.
