A long way from a family that has rooted environmental protection activities on the Chinese frontier (JBpress) - Yahoo! News
(Yuki Kato: Hong Kong company Find Asia corporate consultant) With its vast land area, China is also important in terms of biodiversity, and the number of mammals living is the world's largest, and it is the world's third largest variety of plants.
Some municipalities recognize the value of biodiversity and promote the activities of environmental activists as well as the protection of the environment conducted by local governments.
Sipanna, Yunnan Province, located in southwestern China, is also one of those areas.
His younger sister, Mr. Li, is currently active as an actress in Sisapanna is an area that borders both Laos and Myanmar, and it is an area inhabited by many ethnic minorities, such as the "Thai tribe", which shares its roots with the Thai people of the Kingdom of Thailand.
The source of the Mekong River, which flows through Southeast Asian countries, is flowing.
The area of Sisapanna Thai (Xishuangban-no-Jun) Autonomous Region is only about 0.2 percent of China's total, but about 25 percent of wild animals and 20 percent of species of plants live there.
This time, Mr. Lee, who is engaged in environmental protection activities in Sisapanna, met there and talked about the contents of his activities.
Started environmental protection activities together with my German husband ――Twenty years have passed since I started environmental protection activities.
What was the trigger?
Mr. Lee Our environmental protection activity "Tenji" has been active since the 2000s.
It's my husband, Josef Margraf.
My husband is from Germany and has come to Sisapanna for a joint science project between the United Nations and China.
It is China's only tropical rainforest region, boasting one of the leading biodiversity.
However, at the same time, economic development was delayed.
As a result, large-scale logging of rainforests has progressed in order to root industries in the region and to produce rubber, which had been lacking in China.
My husband was sent here from Germany for the restoration of natural resources and the study of biological resources.
I met and got married when I was a reporter.
Wasn't Mr. Li interested in nature conservation before that?
Mr. Lee I am from Sisapanna, but I had no interest in the importance of tropical rainforests or the meaning of biodiversity.
Therefore, I decided to promote the project with my husband without that knowledge.
