South Korea's Hydrogen Technology Patent, One-fifth of China's "Lagging Delay in Fulfilling the Hydrogen Economy Roadmap" (Chuo Nippo) - Yahoo! News
In 2019, the South Korean government released its hydrogen economy roadmap.
Sixty-five thousand fuel cell vehicles will be used (cumulatively) by 2022, and three hundred and thirty hydrogen stations will be operated across the country.
The target hydrogen price was 6000 won (about 577 yen) per kilometre.
However, on a November basis this year, fuel cell vehicles were only at the target level of 27% (approximately 17,000 units).
Thirty-eight percent (approximately 117 locations) is the target for the dissemination of hydrogen stations in the core infrastructure, and the price of hydrogen per kilometer is not much different from when the roadmap was announced.
It turns out that the government's implementation of the hydrogen economy roadmap is significantly below the target.
On the 12th, the National Federation of Economists (Zenkeiren) released a report titled "The Current State of the Hydrogen Economy and Ecosystems and the Direction of Policy."
According to the report, most of the core components and materials of the hydrogen industry depend on the U.S. and Japan.
Toray (Japan) is the core material of fuel cell vehicles, Kyocera (Japan), Membrane membranes are supplied by DuPont (U.S.A.), Goa (U.S.A.) and 3M (U.S.A.).
The number of hydrogen technology patents in the six leading countries in the global hydrogen market (China, the U.S., the European Union, Japan, South Korea and Germany) has increased by an average of 14% a year (since 2014).
South Korea ranks fifth in the world in terms of the number of patents in the field of hydrogen production and fuel cells (cumulatively 2014-2020).
However, the Zenkeiren said, “In fact, six countries account for the majority of the world’s hydrogen market, and in fact, it means that they are lower among competing countries.”
Patent rankings by country are China, the U.S., the EU, and Japan.
