U.S., aid weapons and ammunition arrives in Ukraine (CNN.co.jp) - Yahoo! News
(CNN) -- U.S. State Department officials reported by Tuesday that equipment, including small firearms and ammunition, had arrived in Ukraine based on a security aid program approved by President Biden.
At the beginning of the year, the last equipment, including four radars to identify launch sites such as mortars, will be transported to Ukraine.
According to U.S. Defense Ministry spokesman Kirby, these aids also included lethal weapons such as the anti-tank "Javelin" anti-tank missiles.
The purpose of military support is to strengthen Ukraine's national defense capabilities in response to the increase in troop forces carried out by Russian forces around the Ukrainian border.
The U.S. is also wary of the Russian military’s moves, talks with members of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) and plans to respond in the event of Russia’s invasion of Ukraine.
On September 1st of this year, President Biden of Ukraine, President Zelensky of Ukraine, when he visited the U.S. White House, had approved weapons assistance.
The U.S. military support for Ukraine reached about $450 million this year.
Since Russia's forced annexation of the Crimean peninsula of Ukraine, the total amount of aid has risen to more than $25 billion.
The U.S. military is also engaged in the training missions of the Ukrainian military.
Last month, in accordance with a multilateral joint training plan to support Ukraine, the U.S. sent the National Guard in Florida.
According to a statement from the U.S. Department of Defense, Europe’s U.S. Special Operations Forces also play a major role in the training and advice of Ukrainian special forces.
The number of U.S. special forces operating in Ukraine has not been identified as a matter of operational secrecy.
On the 7th of this month, President Biden had an online meeting with Russian President Vladimir Putin, but there was also a tense exchange on the situation in Ukraine.
The U.S. is also poised to impose severe economic sanctions in the event of an invasion.
