It’s almost time to throw it away and it’s time to “replace” three items (Harper’s BAZAAR) that tends to be placed in the kitchen for a long period of time (Harper’s Bazaar) - Yahoo! News
Sponge
No matter how often you change it, it probably doesn’t replace it as often as recommended.
According to a recent study, even if a kitchen sponge is sterilized in a microwave oven every day, a lot of bacterium will grow (it is still recommended to sterilize in the microwave).
German researchers who conducted the study recommend replacing the sponge once a week to keep it clean.
The cutting board.
The U.S. Department of Agriculture has pointed out that when it starts to rubberish, scratches and grooves, cutting boards should be replaced.
Once the wounds and grooves progress, it becomes difficult to keep them clean, and the bacteria grow.
Don't forget to disinfect it frequently with a solution that dissolves a tablespoon with an unscented liquid bleach in one gallon (about 3.8 liters) of water.
Soak the surface of the cutting board into the liquid and leave for a few minutes.
Rinse with clean water, dry it naturally or use a clean paper towel to drain and drie.
Chipped or cracked tableware
Disposing of your favorite mug may hurt your chest, but the missing surface becomes a new hotbed for bacteria and germs.
In addition, it can also cause you to cut your lips with missing parts and spill the drink.
That's your chance to buy your new favorite mug!
